			<div class="content" id="post-content-4862204">
									

<p>Hello Galcedion! let's take a look :)</p>
<p><strong>SAND</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: A dead space ship from the future, which has an invisible entity on board that can kill in atmospheric environment and is sentient.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Considering the skip is capable communication, and sounds (at least to extent) self aware. I'd say sapient is the better way to describe the skips intelligence/awarness.</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: The SCP is divided into 2 parts, the ship (SCP-XXXX) and the entity (SCP-XXXX-1). The ship is an advanced human design (not Foundation) that at the current time should not exist. The crew has been killed either by the entity or during venting atmosphere (attempt to kill the entity by the captain). One of the Foundation researchers notes a couple of things about the ship including a device that appears to allow travelling through spacetime using another dimension. Also there is one body missing.</p>
<p>The entity was not on the ship when it engaged its device. It either came from the travel-dimension or said dimension altered a crew member (or both). It can be heard walking around the ship and is pretty harmless in void. It can jump between ships and when in any atmosphere, arms may come from the walls, pulling people &quot;inside&quot; the walls and killing them in the process (they are basically split into their atoms). This is an attempt to pull people into its &quot;dimension&quot;, hinted to be an communication attempt.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>The Foundation team sent to explore the ship finds the original crew dead, some melted to the ships structure. After a while a team member is killed by the entity (accident with suit leads to air escaping) and more die during the escape due to friendly fire and a hull breach. 2 survive and try to communicate with the entity (that can understand visual things like guestures but not writing) while they slowly run out of oxygen and there is no help coming soon.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For the most part, I second cybersqyd here. The narrative did interest me at first, however it turned into a murder story (and a rather cliched one) before anything in the concept could really be explained or expanded upon.- currently this sort of reminds me of alien.</p>
<p>As it stands, the main questions the concept raised are still here (i.e the ships original purpose, the skips initial motives ect.) which makes the narrative feel a bit anti-climactic and unfinished, as there's no real payoff or buildup to anything major.</p>
<p>Another thing I should mention; How do you plan to show all of this info being learned in the narrative? Currently, a lot of this info sounds unobtainable by the Foundation (i.e the skip is only dangerous in the atmosphere, the ship is from the future, the skip wanting to communicate ect.), as there isn't much of a method to learn about the skip or understand and communicate with it (at least not one the Foundation could realistically learn with no prior knowledge of the skip).</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight.</p>
<p>-I would recommend expanding more on the initial questions raised in the concept more, through the narrative. Rather than just turning the narrative into murder monster story with no context or wider explanation. Give something that links everything together, perhaps the skip was part of the reason the ship was here to begin with? maybe the crew were trying to send a warning of some sort? ect.</p>
<p>If you would like me review this (or any concept) further, then please feel free to send me a PM or ask via IRC. Response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques/perspectives in general, then I would highly recommend contacting the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>RED</p>

